"""Original C++23 practice, separating real local support from reference exercises."""
from cpp_lessons import unit, program, UNITS

S='09 Toolchains, evolution, and expert boundaries'
unit(S,'explicit-object23','Explicit object parameters name the receiver',23,
     'An explicit object member function names its receiver parameter in the declaration. That receiver participates in deduction and overload rules, and its value or reference form controls copying and access.|A const reference receiver borrows the existing object for observation. A by-value receiver copies or moves the object according to the call and can work on its own value. This syntax does not create an implicit ownership extension.|Use the receiver form intentionally and avoid returning a borrowed subobject from a temporary receiver. An explicit spelling makes the parameter visible; it does not remove lifetime reasoning.',
     program('', '    Record record{3};\n    std::cout << record.get() << " " << record.value << "\\n";', 'struct Record { int value; int get(this const Record& self) { return self.value; } };\n'),'3 3\n',
     ('Use a by-value receiver that increments its own value before returning it, leaving record unchanged.', 'int get(this const Record& self) { return self.value; }', 'int get(this Record self) { return ++self.value; }', '4 3\n', 'The receiver is copied into self. Incrementing self changes that copy, while the original record still stores three.'),
     'Does an explicit object parameter automatically keep a borrowed receiver alive?', 'No', ['Always','Only when named self'], 'Naming a receiver does not change the underlying value, reference, or lifetime contract.', 'dcl.fct')
unit(S,'forward-like23','forward_like transfers cv and reference intent',23,
     'forward_like<T>(expression) applies a cv/reference pattern based on T to the supplied expression under its rules. It is useful when a generic accessor should mirror a receiver category onto a member.|It does not move a resource by itself or extend the lifetime of the expression. A resulting reference still borrows the same underlying object.|Check constness and lvalue/rvalue category separately. Making a member look consumable is appropriate only when its owner permits that consumption and the referenced storage remains live.',
     program('utility type_traits', '    int value = 7;\n    auto&& selected = std::forward_like<const int&>(value);\n    std::cout << std::is_lvalue_reference_v<decltype(selected)> << " " << std::is_const_v<std::remove_reference_t<decltype(selected)>> << "\\n";'),'1 1\n',
     ('Apply a mutable rvalue-reference pattern while keeping the backing int in scope.', 'std::forward_like<const int&>(value)', 'std::forward_like<int&&>(value)', '0 0\n', 'The selected reference is now an rvalue reference to mutable int. The operation itself has not moved any resource or created a new integer.'),
     'Does forward_like itself transfer a resource?', 'No; it controls the resulting access category', ['Yes, it always empties its operand','It always copies the operand'], 'The later operation selected through that access determines whether copying or moving occurs.', 'forward')
unit(S,'static-call23','A stateless call operator can be static',23,
     'A static operator() provides call syntax without an implicit object parameter. It can express a stateless callable policy and cannot access instance members through an implicit this pointer.|The function still has its ordinary explicit parameters and return contract. Object-call syntax does not imply that an object state is read.|Use a stateful callable when the policy really depends on captured or stored configuration. Choosing static does not automatically produce a measurable performance improvement.',
     program('', '    std::cout << Twice{}(4) << "\\n";', 'struct Twice { static int operator()(int value) { return value * 2; } };\n'),'8\n',
     ('Turn the stateless policy into an increment operation.', 'return value * 2;', 'return value + 1;', '5\n', 'The call still supplies its explicit integer argument. No instance state is involved in either calculation.'),
     'Does this static call operator have an implicit this parameter?', 'No', ['Yes','Only during overload resolution'], 'The static declaration removes an implicit object parameter while retaining the explicit call arguments.', 'over.call')
unit(S,'multi-subscript23','A subscript operator can accept several indices',23,
     'C++23 permits a subscript operator with multiple explicit arguments, allowing a multidimensional interface such as grid[row,column]. The implementation must still define layout and bounds.|The worked grid owns four values and checks each index before multiplying or accessing storage. This small fixed extent makes the mapping easy to inspect.|Do not assume multidimensional syntax supplies automatic bounds checking. A library view and a user-defined operator each have their own access contract.',
     program('array cstddef stdexcept', '    Grid grid;\n    std::cout << grid[1, 0] << "\\n";', 'struct Grid { std::array<int, 4> values{1,2,3,4}; int& operator[](std::size_t row, std::size_t column) { if (row >= 2 || column >= 2) throw std::out_of_range("grid index"); return values[row * 2 + column]; } };\n'),'3\n',
     ('Exercise the invalid-row path and report rejection without accessing an element.', 'std::cout << grid[1, 0] << "\\n";', 'try { (void)grid[2, 0]; } catch (const std::out_of_range&) { std::cout << "bounds\\n"; }', 'bounds\n', 'The guard rejects row two before computing a usable array access. The operator syntax by itself would not have established that safety.'),
     'Who supplies bounds checking for this multidimensional access?', 'The operator implementation', ['The comma spelling automatically checks every index','The preprocessor'], 'The language permits the interface shape while its implementation supplies the access policy.', 'over.sub')
unit(S,'auto-copy23','auto(expression) makes a value conversion explicit',23,
     'The auto(expression) form can express a deduced value conversion. It is useful when an API needs an explicit value snapshot rather than another alias.|In this example the resulting int prvalue initializes a temporary whose lifetime is extended by the local reference binding. That local rule is not a promise about references returned from functions or stored in unrelated objects.|Distinguish taking a value from retaining a reference. Both can be correct, but later mutation of the source makes their behavior visibly different.',
     program('', '    int value = 3;\n    auto&& snapshot = auto(value);\n    value = 7;\n    std::cout << snapshot << " " << value << "\\n";'),'3 7\n',
     ('Replace the independent value snapshot with an alias to value.', 'auto&& snapshot = auto(value);', 'auto& snapshot = value;', '7 7\n', 'The new declaration aliases the original int. The later assignment is visible through both names.'),
     'Does taking an independent int value snapshot make later source writes visible through it?', 'No', ['Always','Only when the new name is a reference'], 'Value conversion and reference binding to the original object have different aliasing semantics.', 'expr.type.conv')

S='07 Standard-library vocabulary and systems facilities'
unit(S,'byteswap23','byteswap reverses integer bytes without defining a wire format',23,
     'byteswap reverses the bytes of an eligible integer representation under its type requirements. It is a named operation for a particular representation transformation.|A byte swap is not by itself a complete endian conversion protocol. A protocol must still define field width and byte order, while the host representation must be handled intentionally.|Use fixed-width unsigned types where the format requires them and check their availability. Do not apply integer byte swapping as a substitute for specifying floating or structured encodings.',
     program('bit cstdint iomanip', '    std::uint32_t value = 0x01020304u;\n    std::cout << std::hex << std::byteswap(value) << "\\n";'),'4030201\n',
     ('Verify that applying the operation twice recovers the original integer.', 'std::hex << std::byteswap(value)', '(std::byteswap(std::byteswap(value)) == value)', '1\n', 'Reversing this representation twice restores the original byte order. The test avoids assuming the host is little-endian.'),
     'Does calling byteswap define a complete portable binary protocol?', 'No', ['Yes','Only if the integer is unsigned'], 'Field widths, endianness, and validation remain properties of the external format.', 'bit.byteswap', [])
unit(S,'to-underlying23','Extract an enum representation without losing its type contract',23,
     'to_underlying returns an enum value converted to its underlying integer type. It documents the representation-level operation without spelling that integer type at the call site.|The conversion does not validate an external integer or make every integer a named enumerator. Parsing and domain checking still belong at the input boundary.|Keep bitmask semantics explicit. A collection of enum values is not automatically a flags algebra merely because an underlying integer can be extracted.',
     program('utility type_traits', '    auto value = std::to_underlying(Flag::ready);\n    std::cout << value << " " << std::is_same_v<decltype(value), unsigned short> << "\\n";', 'enum class Flag : unsigned short { none = 0, ready = 4 };\n'),'4 1\n',
     ('Extract the explicitly named zero enumerator with the same underlying type.', 'Flag::ready', 'Flag::none', '0 1\n', 'The result value changes to zero while the return type remains unsigned short.'),
     'Does to_underlying validate arbitrary input before creating an enum?', 'No', ['Yes','Only for scoped enums'], 'It converts an already supplied enum value to its representation type.', 'utility.underlying')
unit(S,'string-contains23','contains asks a substring question',23,
     'The string and string_view contains operations express whether a character or substring occurs. They are case-sensitive code-unit operations under their specified character traits.|An empty substring is contained at a valid position in an ordinary string. contains does not normalize Unicode or apply an application search locale automatically.|Choose an owning string or borrowed view from the input lifetime. A convenient search operation does not make a dangling string_view valid.',
     program('string', '    std::string text = "alpha:beta";\n    std::cout << text.contains("beta") << " " << text.contains("gamma") << "\\n";'),'1 0\n',
     ('Test a case-changed query and the empty substring.', [('text.contains("beta")', 'text.contains("Beta")'), ('text.contains("gamma")', 'text.contains("")')], '', '0 1\n', 'The uppercase query does not match these lowercase code units. The empty substring does match under the ordinary substring rules.'),
     'Does contains automatically normalize Unicode text or ignore case?', 'No', ['Always','Only for string_view'], 'Encoding-aware normalization and locale-sensitive search require additional policies.', 'string.contains')
unit(S,'resize-overwrite23','Write directly into a string under a bounded callback contract',23,
     'resize_and_overwrite lets an operation fill string storage and return the resulting size under a strict callback contract. This can avoid a separate temporary output buffer.|Write only inside the provided range and return a valid size no greater than the permitted count. Do not read uninitialized positions or throw from a callback whose contract forbids it.|The callback is not a way to retain the pointer after the operation or bypass the string storage rules. Allocation failure and overall API error policy remain separate from filling the characters.',
     program('string cstddef', '    std::string text;\n    text.resize_and_overwrite(5, [](char* data, std::size_t count) noexcept { for (std::size_t i = 0; i < count; ++i) data[i] = char(\'A\' + i); return count; });\n    std::cout << text << "\\n";'),'ABCDE\n',
     ('Return only the first three written characters as the logical result.', 'return count;', 'return count < 3 ? count : std::size_t{3};', 'ABC\n', 'All positions used in the result have been written, and the returned size stays within the provided bound. The string keeps only that logical prefix.'),
     'May the callback return a size larger than its permitted range?', 'No', ['Yes, if capacity is larger','Only when the characters are ASCII'], 'The callback result and access bounds are part of the operation contract.', 'string.capacity')
unit(S,'optional-monadic23','Optional transformations skip absent values',23,
     'transform maps an engaged optional value into a new value, while and_then composes an operation that already returns an optional. Absence propagates without invoking the successful-value callable.|These operations express control flow but do not remove failure or ownership reasoning. The callable must return the intended type and borrowed results must not outlive their owners.|Test both the engaged and absent paths and count side effects when they matter. Do not assume a callback runs merely because the chain expression was written.',
     program('optional', '    int calls = 0;\n    auto result = std::optional<int>{3}.transform([&](int n) { ++calls; return n * 2; }).and_then([](int n) -> std::optional<int> { if (n > 0) return n; return std::nullopt; });\n    std::cout << result.value_or(0) << " " << calls << "\\n";'),'6 1\n',
     ('Start with an absent optional and verify that the transform is skipped.', 'std::optional<int>{3}', 'std::optional<int>{}', '0 0\n', 'The absent state propagates through both operations. The success transform does not increment calls.'),
     'Does optional::transform call its function on an empty optional?', 'No', ['Always','Only if value_or follows'], 'The successful-value function is invoked only for an engaged optional.', 'optional.monadic')
unit(S,'expected-monadic23','Transform success without discarding a typed failure',23,
     'Expected monadic operations compose the success and error paths explicitly. transform maps a successful value, while or_else can handle or transform an error through another expected result.|An error path should retain enough information for the caller to decide what to do. Avoid silently treating an invalid input as a plausible successful default unless that is the documented policy.|Test the path that does not execute the success transform as well as the happy path. Type-level composition does not itself prove that recovery preserves business invariants.',
     program('expected string', '    auto result = input(true).transform([](int n) { return n * 2; }).or_else([](const std::string& error) -> std::expected<int, std::string> { return std::unexpected(error + "!"); });\n    std::cout << (result ? std::to_string(*result) : result.error()) << "\\n";', 'std::expected<int, std::string> input(bool valid) { if (!valid) return std::unexpected(std::string("bad")); return 3; }\n'),'6\n',
     ('Send the error branch through the same chain without inventing a success value.', 'input(true)', 'input(false)', 'bad!\n', 'The success transform is skipped. or_else returns another error result with extra diagnostic context.'),
     'Does transform turn an existing error into a successful value?', 'No; it transforms the successful alternative', ['Always','Only when the error type is string'], 'Success transformation and error recovery are different branches of the result contract.', 'expected.monadic')
unit(S,'ranges-to23','Materialization creates an owning result from a lazy range',23,
     'ranges::to constructs a target container from a range under the selected conversion requirements. It marks a boundary between a lazy borrowed computation and a materialized result.|The resulting vector owns its elements in this example. Clearing the original input after materialization does not remove the result elements, although retained views of that input would need lifetime review.|Account for traversal, conversions, allocation, and element construction. Materializing a range is useful when ownership or repeated access is required, not automatically an optimization.',
     program('ranges vector', '    std::vector<int> input{1, 2, 3};\n    auto result = input | std::views::transform([](int n) { return n * n; }) | std::ranges::to<std::vector>();\n    for (int n : result) std::cout << n << " ";'),'1 4 9 ',
     ('Clear the original container after materialization, then modify the first result element.', 'for (int n : result)', 'input.clear(); result.front() = 9;\n    for (int n : result)', '9 4 9 ', 'The vector result owns independent integer elements. The original lazy pipeline is not retained or used after clearing its source.'),
     'Does the materialized vector here own its integer elements?', 'Yes', ['No, it always stores iterators into input','Only until input is cleared'], 'The range conversion constructs an owning container rather than merely another view.', 'range.utility.conv')
unit(S,'zip23','Zip traversal stops when its shortest input ends',23,
     'A zip view traverses several ranges together. Its ordinary traversal terminates when an input reaches its endpoint, so unequal lengths need an explicit application policy.|The view may expose references into its inputs. Keep those input objects live and respect their invalidation rules while traversing or retaining any result references.|If equal lengths are required, validate that condition separately; shortest-range termination is not a diagnostic for missing records.',
     program('ranges array', '    std::array first{1, 2, 3}; std::array second{10, 20};\n    for (auto [left, right] : std::views::zip(first, second)) std::cout << left + right << " ";'),'11 22 ',
     ('Supply the third matching value so all three pairs are traversed.', 'second{10, 20}', 'second{10, 20, 30}', '11 22 33 ', 'The shortest input now has three elements. No mismatch validation was added; the input lengths simply agree in this case.'),
     'Does ordinary zip iteration reject unequal lengths automatically?', 'No; it ends at the shortest input', ['Yes, by throwing length_error','It always reads to the longest input'], 'The traversal endpoint and application-level length validation are separate contracts.', 'range.zip')
unit(S,'flat-map23','Flat associative storage trades insertion work for locality',23,
     'flat_map provides an ordered associative interface over separate key and mapped containers under its requirements. It differs from a node-based map in storage, insertion, and iterator behavior.|Conditional insertion and assignment are different operations. try_emplace preserves an existing mapped value, while insert_or_assign can replace it.|Choose from measured workload and documented invalidation rules. Dense storage can help locality, but inserting into the middle can require moving many elements.',
     program('flat_map string', '    std::flat_map<int, std::string> values;\n    values.try_emplace(2, "two"); values.try_emplace(1, "one");\n    for (const auto& [key, value] : values) std::cout << key << ":" << value << " ";'),'1:one 2:two ',
     ('Replace the mapped value for the existing key one before iteration.', 'for (const auto& [key, value]', 'values.insert_or_assign(1, "updated");\n    for (const auto& [key, value]', '1:updated 2:two ', 'insert_or_assign intentionally changes an existing mapped value while preserving key ordering. try_emplace would not replace it.'),
     'Can middle insertion into flat storage move many elements?', 'Yes', ['No, every insertion is a node allocation','Only for unordered keys'], 'Storage organization changes insertion and invalidation costs even when lookup interfaces look similar.', 'flat.map')
unit(S,'out-ptr23','Bridge an output pointer into a matching smart owner',23,
     'out_ptr adapts a supported smart pointer to an output-pointer API and transfers the reported pointer into the smart owner according to its contract. The eventual deleter must match the acquisition API.|The worked function is a local C-style output interface implemented in C++; it is not proof of a separately compiled C ABI. It writes null on its ordinary rejection path and allocates with new on success, matching unique_ptr<int> release.|Check the function status and pointer state. Real foreign APIs may have different failure, replacement, aliasing, and allocation policies, which the adapter does not infer automatically.',
     program('memory', '    std::unique_ptr<int> owner;\n    int status = create(std::out_ptr(owner), true);\n    std::cout << status << " " << (owner ? *owner : -1) << "\\n";', 'int create(int** result, bool valid) { *result = nullptr; if (!valid) return 1; *result = new int(7); return 0; }\n'),'0 7\n',
     ('Exercise the ordinary rejection path without allocating a value.', 'std::out_ptr(owner), true', 'std::out_ptr(owner), false', '1 -1\n', 'The function leaves a null output, so the smart owner remains empty. Its deleter is still appropriate for the successful new-int allocation path.'),
     'Does out_ptr choose the correct foreign release function automatically?', 'No; the smart owner must already have the appropriate deleter', ['Always','Only when the API returns an int status'], 'The adapter handles an output-pointer interface, not arbitrary resource-release semantics.', 'out.ptr')


def reference(key,title,prose,prompt,answer,question,correct,wrong,anchor,support):
    unit(S,key,title,23,prose,'','',(prompt,'','','',answer),question,correct,wrong,answer,anchor,[])
    UNITS[-1]['referenceOnly']=True
    UNITS[-1]['support']=support


reference('move-only-function23','A move-only callable wrapper can own noncopyable targets',
          'move_only_function supplies an erased callable wrapper without requiring copying of the wrapper. This permits callable state that owns a unique resource when the target meets the selected signature requirements.|Moving the wrapper transfers its callable ownership; it does not make every captured reference safe. An empty wrapper must be handled according to its documented invocation preconditions.|The local Apple Clang 21/libc++ probe did not provide std::move_only_function. This unit is reference-only; the existing verified unique_ptr and callable lessons establish related prerequisites, not execution of this facility.',
          'Design a queued callable that owns a unique resource and explain why a copy-requiring wrapper is inappropriate.',
          'Use a move-only wrapper or an explicit move-only command type whose target owns the resource by value. Move it into the queue, invoke only a valid target, and let one final owner release it. Keep borrowed dependencies live separately and define rejection and cancellation behavior.',
          'Does a move-only wrapper make a borrowed reference capture safe indefinitely?', 'No', ['Yes','Only after moving the wrapper'], 'func.wrap.move',
          'Reference only: std::move_only_function was unavailable in the Apple Clang 21/libc++ C++23 syntax probe; no runnable claim.')
reference('enumerate-chunk23','Enumeration and chunk views need input and remainder policies',
          'Enumeration pairs a position with a traversed element, while chunking groups consecutive subranges. These solve different range-composition tasks and retain the requirements of the underlying ranges.|A final chunk can be shorter than the requested group size. Input-only and multipass ranges have different iterator and reuse constraints, so a view type is not a promise of arbitrary repeated access.|The local library probe lacked views::enumerate and views::chunk. This is a reference-only design exercise; do not substitute an indexed loop and label the standard-library facility verified.',
          'Specify a chunk-processing algorithm for empty input, exact multiples, and a short final group; state whether groups borrow the source.',
          'Define a positive chunk size, allow zero groups for empty input, and process the actual size of each group rather than assuming the final group is full. Keep the source live while borrowed groups are used; materialize owned groups if they must outlive it. Check whether the chosen range supports repeated traversal.',
          'Must every final chunk contain the requested number of elements?', 'No', ['Always','Only when the input size is odd'], 'range.chunk',
          'Reference only: views::enumerate and views::chunk were unavailable in the local C++23 syntax probes.')
reference('standard-generator23','Standard generator support is distinct from a teaching generator',
          'The C++23 generator facility is a coroutine-based range with its own promise, iterator, allocation, and yielded-reference contracts. A hand-written generator wrapper is not a conforming replacement merely because both use co_yield.|Reason about the frame owner and the lifetime of each yielded value or reference. Single-pass traversal and suspended state can invalidate assumptions borrowed from a vector.|The installed library lacked the generator header. The separately checked coroutine project verifies its own explicit wrapper contract, while this standard-library unit remains reference-only.',
          'Compare the verified teaching Generator project with a standard generator API without claiming they have identical iterator or allocation contracts.',
          'List the actual ownership, exception, resume, and destruction behavior of the project. Then audit the standard facility requirements separately, including yield-reference validity, iterator category, allocation, and nested generation. Reuse conceptual reasoning but require a real supported implementation before claiming standard-library execution.',
          'Does a passing custom generator establish conformance of std::generator?', 'No', ['Yes','Only if both use co_yield'], 'coro.generator',
          'Reference only: the generator header was unavailable in the local Apple Clang 21/libc++ C++23 probe.')
reference('spanstream23','A span-backed stream has bounded external storage',
          'Span-backed streams operate on a supplied character range rather than owning an independently growing string buffer. They still use stream state and formatted extraction/insertion contracts.|Check capacity failure and distinguish produced characters from unused buffer storage. The backing character array must remain live while the stream or returned views can access it.|The installed library lacked the spanstream header. This reference exercise extends the verified span and stream-state lessons but is not recorded as runnable support for this header.',
          'Design checks for writing a formatted message into a fixed span with insufficient room, exact room, and extra capacity.',
          'Keep the buffer owner live, check stream state after writing, and consume only the reported written range. Test capacity boundaries and define whether partial output is discarded or reported. Do not assume the entire backing array contains initialized message text or a null terminator.',
          'Does a span-backed stream own and extend the backing array lifetime?', 'No', ['Always','Only for output streams'], 'spanstream',
          'Reference only: the spanstream header was unavailable in the local C++23 probe.')
reference('stacktrace23','Stack traces are diagnostic data with platform limits',
          'Stacktrace facilities can capture a representation of an execution call sequence when supported by the implementation. Availability, symbolization, allocation, and frame detail can vary by build and platform.|A trace is diagnostic evidence rather than a proof of causality, object ownership, or universal memory safety. Avoid assuming that every signal or low-memory handler can safely capture or format one.|The installed library lacked the stacktrace header. This unit remains reference-only, and no trace payload or private memory content is committed.',
          'Define a bounded error-reporting policy that can omit a trace when capture or symbolization is unavailable.',
          'Preserve a useful error code and operation context independently of optional tracing. Capture and symbolize only in a permitted context, bound stored diagnostics, redact sensitive paths when needed, and tolerate missing frames. Keep raw diagnostic payloads in ignored development artifacts.',
          'Does a captured stack trace prove an object lifetime or memory leak property?', 'No', ['Always','Only with debug symbols'], 'stacktrace',
          'Reference only: the stacktrace header was unavailable in the local C++23 probe.')
